# Cloudflare Pages reads this file from the site root and applies these response
# headers. No build step, no tooling — Pages parses it directly.
# Syntax: a URL pattern on its own line, then indented "Header: value" lines.
# Docs: https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/headers/
#
# Added 2026-08-20. Nothing here changes how the site looks or behaves; these
# are instructions to the browser about how to treat the page.
#
# All commentary is kept OUTSIDE the rule block below: Cloudflare documents `#`
# comments on their own lines, but does not document them inside a block, and a
# live site is the wrong place to find out.
#
# What each header does:
#
#   X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
#     Don't guess a file's type from its contents — trust the declared type.
#     Stops a file being treated as a script when it isn't.
#
#   X-Frame-Options: DENY
#     Don't let another site load these pages inside a frame on their own
#     domain. Prevents the site being wrapped and passed off as something else.
#
#   Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
#     When a visitor clicks away, send only the domain as the referrer, not the
#     full path — and send nothing at all when downgrading to http.
#
#   Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(), payment=(), usb=()
#     This site asks for none of these. Deny them explicitly so nothing embedded
#     or injected can either.
#
# Deliberately NOT set:
#
#   Content-Security-Policy
#     Would need 'unsafe-inline' anyway: about.html uses an inline onerror
#     handler on the headshot (the fallback for when Cloudflare's image resizing
#     isn't available), and both pages run an inline <head> script to set the
#     theme before paint. A CSP permissive enough to allow those buys little,
#     and a stricter one silently breaks the image fallback and flashes the
#     wrong theme. Revisit only with a *.pages.dev preview to test against.
#
#   Strict-Transport-Security
#     Set this in the Cloudflare dashboard (SSL/TLS → Edge Certificates → HSTS),
#     not here. Browsers cache HSTS for its full max-age, so a mistake is hard
#     to undo; the dashboard keeps it reversible.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(), payment=(), usb=()
